 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — a failure inside the check aborts it with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22. The maintainer overrode §check-fails-open (in force from 2026-09-11) and replaced it with this
+  decision.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit, which blocks the commit.
+  Large staged files still only produce a warning; the check ends in `exit 0` once it gets there.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`; a statement shaped `cond && cmd` (when `cond` is false it returns 1, so the enclosing loop
+  fails and the commit is blocked for no reason); running the per-file loop inside `$(...)` or a pipe that feeds into
+  `$(...)`, where bash turns `-e` off and failures are ignored.
+- Rejected: §check-fails-open (`set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, always `exit 0`), overridden by the maintainer.
+- Reason: the maintainer wants a failing command to stop the check instead of being ignored. The 2026-09-10 incident
+  behind the old decision (a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked every commit for a day) cannot happen
+  this way: `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` skips files that no longer exist before `wc` runs.
